course: dictionary
lesson: 11255
title: Worthy: deserving praise
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Worthy means deserving something, such as respect, praise or a prize. A worthy winner has earned the win. Worthy of is followed by the thing that is deserved: a team can be worthy of a trophy.
explain: Look for the clue words around it. In «She was a worthy winner, and everyone clapped», the clapping shows that people think she earned her win.
explain: Be careful with «A worthy fellow, Ratty, with many good qualities, but very little intelligence and absolutely no education.» The word but shows a mixed picture: Ratty is praised first, then his faults follow. Context helps you see that the author is not giving full praise.
explain: Worthy is also a building block in longer words: trustworthy, newsworthy and seaworthy. When you see worthy at the end of a word, think about what it is worthy of, and the meaning often follows.
fact: Worthy means deserving of respect, praise or attention.
fact: «Worthy of» means deserving something, as in «worthy of a prize».
fact: A worthy cause is good and useful, but it can sometimes be a little dull.
fact: As a noun, «the local worthies» is an old or jokey way to say the important people in an area.
fact: Trustworthy means able to be trusted, and seaworthy means safe to sail.
exercise: «The charity raised money for a worthy cause.» What does worthy mean here? | Full of water | Heavy and slow | Very loud | *Good and deserving of support
exercise: «The young swimmer was worthy of the gold medal.» What does worthy of mean? | *Deserving it | Afraid of it | Tired of it | Lost in it
exercise: Toad hopes his boats are seaworthy. What does seaworthy mean? | Too big for the river | *Safe to sail | Very slow | Made of wood
test: «She was a worthy winner, and everyone clapped.» Which word tells you that she earned the praise? | winner | everyone | *worthy | clapped
test: In «the local worthies», what are the worthies? | Old trees on the hill | *Important people in the area | Animals in the local park | Shops in the town
exam: «A worthy fellow, Ratty, with many good qualities, but very little intelligence and absolutely no education.» What does the word but tell us? | Ratty has left the story | The author is about to praise Ratty even more | The author is saying Ratty is famous | *The author adds faults after the praise
